# Build a Camunda 8.9 AI Agent connector runtime with gateway authentication

Build a Spring Boot connector runtime that hosts Camunda's **standard, unmodified AI Agent
connector** (Task + Sub-process) and changes exactly one thing: the AWS Bedrock provider must
authenticate to an internal "organization Bedrock gateway" with HTTP headers
(`x-bam-token: <token>` plus configurable `Accept` and `Host`) instead of AWS SigV4. Every other
provider (Anthropic, Azure OpenAI, Vertex AI, OpenAI, OpenAI-compatible) must behave identically to
the stock connector.

Hard constraint: no fork, no patched classes, no reflection, no AOP. Integrate only through
Camunda's documented Spring extension points. Agent orchestration, tools, MCP, memory, prompts and
element semantics stay Camunda's code.

## Build setup

- Maven + wrapper, Java 21, `groupId com.anthrobyte.camunda`,
  `artifactId org-ai-agent-connector-runtime`, base package `com.anthrobyte.camunda.aiagent`.
- Import `io.camunda.connector:connector-parent` (version 8.9.12) as a BOM and take **every**
  transitive version from it, so this project never upgrades something Camunda didn't test with.
  Hold the connectors version in one property — upgrading Camunda should mean editing one line.
- Depend on `spring-boot-starter-camunda-connectors` and `connector-agentic-ai` with scope
  `provided` (the Camunda runtime supplies them when the thin jar is dropped in), and build both a
  thin jar and a standalone `*-exec.jar`.
- Run `*Test` and `*IT` in the same surefire execution: the suite must need no external services.

## Integration approach

Investigate how Camunda's agentic-ai auto-configuration builds chat models and find the seam that
lets an application supply its own factory without modifying Camunda. Register your replacement so
Camunda's default backs off, and make it a thin router: Bedrock configurations go to your own
builder, everything else to a factory constructed exactly like Camunda's default bean.

Camunda's Bedrock client setup lives partly in inaccessible methods. Where you must reproduce it
(timeout derivation, proxy-aware Apache HTTP client, model-parameter mapping), keep **all** of it in
one clearly-marked class that names its upstream origin, and add a test that compares the request
body your model sends against the one Camunda's own factory produces.

## Layering

Keep four packages with strict dependency direction:

- **auth** — credentials only; knows nothing of Camunda, HTTP transport or AWS.
- **transport** — an AWS SDK HTTP client decorator; knows nothing of Camunda.
- **camunda** — the only Camunda-facing code.
- **config** — auto-configuration and typed properties under your own prefix
  (`organization.ai-gateway.auth.*`), never under `camunda.*`, so a future release can't clash.

## Authentication

A provider abstraction returns the headers for the next request and can invalidate rejected ones.
Ship four modes: a **placeholder unsigned JWT** (so the runtime can be wired end-to-end before real
token generation exists — it must warn loudly at startup), **OAuth2 client credentials**, **static
headers**, and **custom** (an application-supplied token source or provider bean).

The token cache is the part that needs care:

- lock-free fast path while the cached token is valid;
- single-flight refresh, so a burst of concurrent jobs causes one token request, not hundreds;
- threads waiting on a refresh that failed share that failure instead of retrying serially;
- bounded waiting, so a hung token endpoint can't pin job worker threads;
- treat a token as expired slightly before it really is, capping the skew at half the lifetime;
- invalidate by identity, so concurrent 401s don't each trigger a refresh.

Separate **permanent** from **transient** failures and map them onto LangChain4j's non-retriable and
retriable exception types, so Camunda's existing job-retry and incident semantics are unchanged.
Failure messages carry a reason code and optional HTTP status, never a cause, never a response body,
never anything echoed back from an upstream that could contain a secret.

## Transport

Decorate the AWS SDK's HTTP client so the gateway headers are attached on **every** attempt. Work
out which SDK hook actually runs per attempt after signing — an interceptor that runs once per
execution is the wrong one — and document why in the architecture notes.

The decorator must: configure the Bedrock client so no signer can ever run (anonymous credentials
plus an auth-scheme resolution that only offers no-auth) and strip AWS signing headers as defence in
depth; replace same-named headers and leave everything else untouched; retry **once** on 401 after
invalidating, but only when the provider can actually produce different credentials; return all
other statuses to the SDK unchanged; and fail closed if credentials can't be obtained.

Also fetch credentials before the chat call itself, so a credential failure surfaces as a sanitized
error with nothing sent and outside LangChain4j's retry loop. A final gateway 401/403 becomes a
sanitized authentication failure; every other error (LLM 4xx, gateway 5xx, timeouts) is rethrown
unchanged.

## Endpoint handling

The gateway URL is **not** a runtime setting: each AI Agent element carries it as its custom
endpoint and the runtime uses it as configured — no allow-list, no scheme restriction. Only a
missing or unusable URL fails, and it fails closed with no fallback to AWS credentials. Warn at
startup that any URL an element points at will therefore receive the organization credentials, and
warn per-job when element AWS credentials or an API key are present and ignored.

## Configuration

Ship an `application.yml` with no secrets — only `${ENV_VAR}` placeholders — that:

- gives the AI Agent Task and Sub-process **custom job types** (`ORG_AI_AGENT_TASK_TYPE`,
  `ORG_AI_AGENT_SUBPROCESS_TYPE`), because the standard types are already polled by the default
  runtime, and warns at startup if a standard type is still in use;
- disables the other connectors bundled in `connector-agentic-ai`, which would otherwise poll their
  standard job types too;
- exposes the auth settings as `ORG_AI_GATEWAY_*` / `ORG_AI_*` variables, with the `Host` header
  defaulting to an obviously-fake placeholder that the runtime warns about.

Validate configuration at startup, reporting **all** problems at once by property name and the
environment variable that maps to it, never by value. Reject a plain-http token URL unless an
explicit local-development flag is set, and reject credentials embedded in URLs.

Log a single startup summary line (versions, job types, auth mode). Override the starter's logback
config so structured key/value fields survive the console pattern, and add an opt-in JSON profile.

## Element templates

Don't hand-write templates. Write a script that downloads Camunda's official AI Agent templates for
the pinned connectors version and applies a small, reviewable set of edits: new id and name, the
custom job type, provider fixed to Bedrock, Bedrock auth pinned and its credential fields removed
(the runtime supplies credentials), custom endpoint made required. Everything else stays as Camunda
ships it. Record what the template was derived from, and commit the generated output.

## Packaging

Multi-stage Dockerfile on Temurin 21 with a cached dependency layer and Spring Boot layer
extraction; non-root runtime image; no secrets baked in. Example Kubernetes manifests: a ConfigMap
of non-secret settings, a placeholder Secret created out-of-band, and a hardened Deployment
(non-root, read-only root filesystem, dropped capabilities, actuator probes).

## Tests

Around a hundred tests, all offline. Build a small programmable HTTP server on the JDK's built-in
server to stand in for the token endpoint, the Bedrock gateway and an OpenAI-compatible backend, and
a harness that boots Camunda's **real** agentic-ai auto-configuration alongside yours, mocking only
cluster infrastructure.

Prove, at minimum: the expected headers go out and no SigV4 artifact does; element AWS credentials
are ignored; tokens are cached, refreshed on expiry, and a 401 causes exactly one retry; a token
outage fails closed with no request sent; concurrent refresh collapses to one token request; the
request body matches Camunda's own Bedrock factory; non-Bedrock traffic is byte-identical to the
stock connector; disabling the feature restores Camunda's own beans; configuration errors fail fast
without leaking values; and a full run at TRACE level never logs a token, client secret or AWS key.

## Documentation

A README that explains the one thing that changed and links five docs: architecture (execution path,
the extension point, why it's safe, a walkthrough of one request — include diagrams), configuration
reference, deployment including the hybrid-SaaS and Modeler story, an upgrade procedure that lists
each assumption about Camunda alongside the test that trips when it breaks, and a security review
with a threat/control/test matrix.

Ground the architecture notes in the actual resolved artifacts rather than assumptions: verify every
class and method you name exists in the versions the build resolves.

## Cross-cutting rules

Never log a credential — header names, hosts, paths, statuses and reason codes only, and redact in
every `toString`. Fail closed. Secrets come from the environment, never from BPMN, process
variables, the image or the repo. Document *why* rather than *what*, and note the upgrade tripwire
wherever you reproduce Camunda behaviour.
